  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "**Result (Section 6).** These are the orders of the ε-expansion needed to fix the CG state ψ₀ = lim ε^k Ψ_B.\n- **Data.** Site1 + site2 + Ω rows for 8 values of w, plus the x₀ normalization.\n- **Meaning of N_min.** Each data function ⟨c|Ψ_B⟩ is needed through relative order ε^{N_min} beyond the leading ε^{−k}, i.e. through absolute order ε^{N_min−k}.\n\n| μ | mult | k (Ψ ~ ε^{−k}) | N_min |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [6,4,0] | 1 | 4 | 0 |\n| [6,3,1] | 1 | 5 | 1 |\n| [5,5,0] | 1 | 5 | 1 |\n| [5,4,1] | 2 | 6 | 1 |\n| [6,2,2] | 1 | 6 | 2 |\n| [5,3,2] | 2 | 7 | 2 |\n| [4,4,2] | 1 | 8 | 2 |\n| [4,3,3] | 1 | 9 | **5** |\n\n- **The prior doesn't matter.** The prior ψ₀ ∈ HW_μ does not change N_min in any sector.\n- **Clean numerics.** The null spaces are exact: the next singular value at the cut is exactly 0.\n- **Accuracy.** The x₀ row uses a stable factorized inverse, and the old `Inverse` lost all precision at complex ε. The M_j resum to 3.5×10⁻¹⁸ at ε = 1/40.\n- **In short:**\n  - the top irrep needs only the leading order;\n  - most irreps need 1–2 subleading orders;\n  - the bottom irrep [4,3,3] needs 5.\n- **Caveat.** The numbers are for 8 values of w. With fewer w they could only grow; with more w they cannot drop below what one extra w adds (not tested).",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## 7. Dependence on the ket twist direction φ\nω only moves the bra, whereas φ moves the eigenstates Ψ_B themselves. The question is whether the ε → 0 limit, i.e. the CG basis, depends on φ.\n- **Multiplicity 1:** no. The limit is the unique HW vector.\n- **Multiplicity 2:** the limit selects a basis of HW_μ, which could in principle depend on φ, but only through s = (φ₁−φ₂)/(φ₂−φ₃), since shifts and rescalings of φ are trivial.\n\n**What the cell does.**\n- It computes the limit directions c₂/c₁, in the orthonormal HW basis, of both eigenstates in [5,4,1] and [5,3,2], at ε = 10⁻⁹ for four values of φ. These include φ and 2φ + (1,1,1) as a sanity check.\n- It compares them with the eigenvectors of the **untwisted** inhomogeneous transfer matrix t₁ + πt₂ (u = 3/7, z = 1) restricted to HW_μ. That restriction is invariant, since the untwisted t's commute with gl(3).",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* limit directions c2/c1 (orthonormal HW basis) of both eigenstates in the multiplicity-2 sectors, as functions of φ *)\nmixRatio[φv_] := Block[{φT = φv}, With[{sts = psiStatesP[10^-9, 120]}, Table[With[{st = Select[sts, #[\"mu\"] == μ &]}, Sort[Table[With[{c = hwB[μ] . s0[\"ψ\"]}, N[c[[2]]/c[[1]], 12]], {s0, st}], Re[#1] < Re[#2] &]], {μ, {{5, 4, 1}, {5, 3, 2}}}]]];\nφList = {{1/3, -2/7, 5/11}, 2 {1/3, -2/7, 5/11} + {1, 1, 1}, {1/3, 1/5, -3/4}, {0, 1, 3}};\nsOf[φv_] := (φv[[1]] - φv[[2]])/(φv[[2]] - φv[[3]]);\nmr = mixRatio /@ φList;\n(* untwisted chain: t1 + π t2 at z = 1 restricted to HW_μ; HW_μ is invariant (residual) *)\nmixU = mixSp[60, N[{1, 1, 1}, 60]];\nuntw = Table[With[{H = N[hwB[μ], 60]}, {Sort[N[#[[2]]/#[[1]], 12] & /@ Eigenvectors[H . mixU . Transpose[H]], Re[#1] < Re[#2] &], N[Norm[Flatten[mixU . Transpose[H] - Transpose[H] . (H . mixU . Transpose[H])]], 3]}], {μ, {{5, 4, 1}, {5, 3, 2}}}];\nchecks[\"ε->0 limit in multiplicity sectors independent of φ (max spread over 4 φ, O(ε) expected)\"] = Max[Abs[Flatten[Table[mr[[i]] - mr[[1]], {i, 2, 4}]]]];\nchecks[\"ε->0 limit = untwisted transfer-matrix eigenbasis on HW_μ (max diff, O(ε) expected)\"] = Max[Abs[Flatten[untw[[All, 1]] - mr[[1]]]]];\nGrid[Join[{{\"φ\", \"s\", \"[5,4,1]: c2/c1 of both states\", \"[5,3,2]: c2/c1 of both states\"}}, Table[{φList[[i]], sOf[φList[[i]]], N[mr[[i, 1]], 8], N[mr[[i, 2]], 8]}, {i, 4}],\n   {{\"untwisted eigenbasis\", \"z = 1\", N[untw[[1, 1]], 8], N[untw[[2, 1]], 8]}, {\"HW_μ invariance residual\", \"\", untw[[1, 2]], untw[[2, 2]]}}], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
      {
